## ===========================================================================
## THE BALL — Book 12 Parts 5-7 (`story.ni:11048-11893`) under the redesigned
## ending, Chapter 11.
##
## The change document ("Chapter 11 — The Ball (the redesigned ending)",
## 2026-09-03) supersedes the dance and cuts Book 13: the ending is the
## Ballroom, then Vella. Chapter 11 runs from the step into the ballroom
## to the Priestess leading Jacqueline out. What the source supplies —
## the room, its scenery, the guests, the quartet, the servants, the six
## trees and their speakers, the Queen's look, Bobby's and the Baron's
## descriptions — carries as written; what David's design adds — the
## Baron's confrontation and dismissal, the mercenaries barred, the throne
## with Bobby and Pieter beside it, Bobby's conversation, the Queen's
## greeting, the judgment, the Priestess and her asking, the losing ending —
## is built as TRIGGERS WITH PLACEHOLDERS, the world consequence of each
## live and the lines David's. Conversations are STUBS under the standing
## rule.
##
## THE RULINGS (change document, Chapter 11):
##   * THE SHAPE — ballroom only, no war room, no dance; free conversation
##     with every major player while the Queen watches. The Baron confronts
##     her early, is dismissed, and his men are barred — then the room opens
##     (gap 1's reading, stated and not corrected).
##   * EXTENT — the world of light to the Priestess leading her out; Vella is
##     Chapter 12 (Phase 11's). Chapter XII's row begins on her first visit
##     to Vella, a PLACEHOLDER room here.
##   * THE BARON BEFORE THE COURT — the letter produced, the Queen's threat
##     quiet, the Baron let go; nothing burned, nothing confessed aloud.
##   * BOBBY — revealed beside the throne, the Queen's man; the War Room's
##     reveals move to his conversation (a stub).
##   * THE JUDGMENT — the claim, the conversations her evidence, teeth: fail
##     and she rises and leaves with Bobby, the room turns its back, the
##     game ends; the verdict is never spoken; won, she nods the Priestess
##     toward Jacqueline. THE MEASURE (gap 2, Phase 10's to set and report):
##     seven players count — Jacobs, the Princess, the Duke, Amhyron, Bresa,
##     the Prince, Bobby — one point each for the first conversation; FIVE
##     of the seven is a winning showing.
##   * THE PRIESTESS — on her own time, blocked on the Queen's decision;
##     she asks, and Jacqueline's spirit is drawn to follow. THE CLOCK (gap
##     8, Phase 10's to set and report): she arrives on the twenty-fourth
##     turn after the room opens; the Queen's patience runs out on the
##     thirty-sixth. Evidence in hand when the Priestess is present decides
##     it — arrive first and she waits.
##   * THE CRESCENDO — no fail state after the nod.
##   * WHO IS IN THE ROOM — Pieter beside the Queen (lost on entry, found
##     there); the Prince stays and is there from the start; the Queen has a
##     greeting; the Baron early then gone; the Princess a ballgoer.
##
## STUBS TAKING THE SOURCE'S PATH (flagged, per the standing rule's item 4):
## `show letter to baron`, `give letter to baron` and `talk to baron` are
## the confrontation's one move — the dismissal placeholder, the Baron gone,
## the room opened. `talk to <player>` prints the tree's TODO once and its
## return TODO after; the Princess leaves after hers as PR15 has her.
##
## GAPS (change document): the opening's order (built as the reading); the
## judgment's measure and the Priestess's clock (set above, reported); how
## Pieter came to the throne and whether he speaks (his tree answers, as in
## Chapter 9); the dagger drawn nowhere; the letter's holder (she keeps it);
## the mercenaries barred offstage and heard; the Queen's Guard as a line in
## the placeholder, not an entity; the losing ending's, the greeting's and
## the asking's lines — David's.
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * "You can't see Pieter anywhere" — the source prints it on her first
##     reference to him; Chord cannot intercept a reference to an absent
##     thing, so the line closes the arrival text instead. The words are
##     Gentry's; the moment moved (deviation 12 in `secret-letter.story`).
##   * Bobby's Skirmish description carries without "even as he parries the
##     Baron's savage blows" — the clause names the cut scene (deviation
##     13).
##   * The distant descriptions are the presence lines; the Queen's, which
##     is her only description, is her examine with its first-time tail.
##   * The Baron's "stands between you and the fireplace" (the War Room).
##   * `kiss`, `throw`, `exit`/`out` (no actions; south is the exit).
##   * The source's `[if JE1 is fired]` roster in `talk to guests` gates on
##     the room being open, which is when Jacobs has spoken.
##   * The Prince's GR1 opened unasked in Brief Encounter with the men at
##     the doors; under the ruling he is a player she approaches, and GR7
##     loses the hand on her shoulder.
##   * The source's themes and the `pause`.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE BALL — the state the room and the court read. `pending` on entry;
## `confrontation` from the Baron's approach; `dismissed` the turn the
## letter is produced (a pivot — the room's own clause does the opening);
## `open` from then; `decided` from the Queen's nod; `taken` when the
## Priestess leads her out; `abandoned` is the losing ending.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the ball
  aka the court, fossville's ball
  proper
  states: pending, confrontation, dismissed, open, decided, taken, abandoned
  counter turns starts 0 between 0 and 40

  Fossville's ball.

  when the player's stepping-in expires
    move Baron Fossville to the Ballroom
    move the Queen to the Ballroom
    phrase confrontation-stub
    change the ball to confrontation
  end when

  when the player's drawn expires
    phrase priestess-asks-stub
    change the ball to taken
    move the player to Vella
  end when

define timer stepping-in for the player
  crossing
end timer

## The turn between the nod and the asking. A room's every-turn phrase is
## dropped when she has left the room by the turn's end, so the asking and
## the leaving ride a clock on the court, as the raid's capture did.

define timer drawn for the player
  following
end timer

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE BALLROOM (`story.ni:11052-11073`). The first description is the
## world of light, the room, the breathtaking tail and Pieter lost.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the Ballroom
  a room
  aka ball room, hall, ball, party, lights, glittering lights
  south is blocked: ballroom-press-of-people
  north is blocked: ballroom-cant-see-the-edges
  east is blocked: ballroom-cant-see-the-edges
  west is blocked: ballroom-cant-see-the-edges
  northeast is blocked: ballroom-cant-see-the-edges
  northwest is blocked: ballroom-cant-see-the-edges
  southeast is blocked: ballroom-cant-see-the-edges
  southwest is blocked: ballroom-cant-see-the-edges
  first time
    You step into a world of light...

    Hundreds of candles spread an unearthly golden glow throughout the hall.
    From massive chandeliers dripping with crystal, from golden candelabras
    ensconced in the walls, the multitude of flames flicker like stars. The
    ballroom is packed with people; all of Toresal's high society seems to
    be here tonight, and still the enormous hall does not seem full.
    Servants navigate through the crowds, carrying silver platters loaded
    with pastries and canapés. Somewhere, a string quartet is playing a
    lively concerto.

    It's breathtaking. It's every fantasy of beauty and riches that you ever
    dreamed of, brought to life.

    You can't see Pieter anywhere; he must have gotten separated from you in
    the press of guests. For the time being at least, you're on your own.

  Hundreds of candles spread an unearthly golden glow throughout the hall.
  From massive chandeliers dripping with crystal, from golden candelabras
  ensconced in the walls, the multitude of flames flicker like stars. The
  ballroom is packed with people; all of Toresal's high society seems to be
  here tonight, and still the enormous hall does not seem full. Servants
  navigate through the crowds, carrying silver platters loaded with pastries
  and canapés. Somewhere, a string quartet is playing a lively concerto.
  {throne-stub}

  after the player entering
    first time
      move Pieter offstage
      change Pieter to lost
      change the preparations to ball
      start the player's stepping-in
  end after

  on every turn while the ball is dismissed
    move Baron Fossville offstage
    move Bobby here
    change Bobby to court
    move Pieter here
    change Pieter to throne
    move Jacobs the Elder here
    move the Princess here
    move the Duke of Inhyron here
    move the Duchess of Inhyron here
    move the Baron of Amhyron here
    move the Earl of Bresa here
    move the Prince of Gravesal here
    phrase jacobs-je1-stub
    change the ball to open
  end on

  on every turn while the ball is open
    raise the ball's turns by 1
  end on

  on every turn while the ball is open and the ball's turns is 24
    move the Vedd Priestess here
    phrase priestess-arrives-stub
  end on

  on every turn while the ball is open and the Vedd Priestess is here and the Queen's evidence is at least 5
    phrase queen-rises-and-nods-stub
    move the Queen offstage
    move Bobby offstage
    move Pieter offstage
    start the player's drawn
    change the ball to decided
  end on

  on every turn while the ball is open and the ball's turns is at least 36 and the Queen's evidence is less than 5
    change the ball to abandoned
    lose queen-leaves-stub
  end on

## `story.ni:11065`, with the source's `[first time]` aside.

define phrase ballroom-press-of-people, first-time
  The press of people is so close, you can't make your way back to the Foyer
  without rudely pushing your way through at least a dozen conversations.
  Besides, you can't leave without Pieter. Where did he get off to...?
or
  The press of people is so close, you can't make your way back to the Foyer
  without rudely pushing your way through at least a dozen conversations.
  Besides, you can't leave without Pieter.
end phrase

## `story.ni:11067`, the facings as blocked exits.

define phrase ballroom-cant-see-the-edges
  The guests are crowded so close together, you can't even see to the edges
  of the room.
end phrase

## `story.ni:11076`

define phrase ballroom-overwhelming
  The constant wave of voices, conversations, laughter, and music is
  overwhelming.
end phrase

