## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CONFRONTATION AND THE DISMISSAL (David: "There is a confrontation
## with the Baron, but he's dismissed by the Queen, tries to bring his
## mercenaries in, but they are barred by the Queen's Guard"; "The letter
## is produced, the Queen already knows of The Baron's guilt and quietly
## threatens him, but allows him to leave instead"). Two placeholders: the
## Baron's approach one turn after her entry, and the dismissal when she
## produces the letter or speaks to him. The mercenaries are barred
## offstage and heard (gap 6); the Queen's Guard is a line (gap 7); she
## keeps the letter (gap 5).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase confrontation-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — The crowd parts and Baron Fossville comes
  through it straight to you, before anyone else has spoken a word. The court
  turns to watch. 'Lady Jacqueline,' he says, loud enough for the room. 'A
  word. I believe you have something of mine.' He holds out his hand for the
  letter, and behind him, on her throne, the Queen watches and says nothing.)
end phrase

define phrase dismissal-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — You hold the letter up where the court can see
  it: the Duke's own hand, and your own name in it. The Queen does not raise
  her voice. 'Baron,' she says, 'I know what you did. Go, while I am still
  content to let you.' Fossville's face goes white, then red; he turns and
  shouts for his men, and beyond the doors there is a scuffle and the flat
  voices of the Queen's Guard, and the doors do not open. He goes. The room
  lets out its breath, and opens to you: the Queen on her throne, and beside
  it Bobby, alive, and Pieter.)
end phrase

## The Baron at the ball (`story.ni:9653`, the `otherwise` branch), with
## its first-time tail; his block is in `gallows.chord`.

define phrase baron-at-the-ball, first-time
  Now that you're up close, the cracks in Fossville's façade show themselves
  clearly. His thick, black hair is disheveled. His face is haggard. He's
  tried to hide the lines and the dark circles under his eyes with extra
  make-up, but it just makes him look more ghoulish. With some
  satisfaction, you realize that you must have been really running the
  Baron ragged the last couple of days. No wonder he hasn't made an
  appearance at his own party.
or
  Now that you're up close, the cracks in Fossville's façade show themselves
  clearly. His thick, black hair is disheveled. His face is haggard. He's
  tried to hide the lines and the dark circles under his eyes with extra
  make-up, but it just makes him look more ghoulish.
end phrase

## His platform line (`story.ni:9660`) is the square's marker now, since a
## presence line cannot vary (GH #364) and he stands here too.

define phrase fossville-on-the-platform, stopping while Baron Fossville is in the Lord's Market
  Baron Fossville stands on the platform, addressing the crowd.
end phrase

